          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "A cough that lasts two to three weeks after a cold is common. The common-cold summary says cough \"frequently outlasts everything else, persisting two to three weeks, which is normal\" (Upper respiratory infection (common cold)). The antibiotic stewardship guidance says the same: cough alone lasting two to three weeks after a cold is expected, and it is not by itself a sign of a bacterial infection (Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated).\n\nNo emergency warning signs matched your description. That does not rule out a problem, though. The safety check only covers a fixed list of presentations.\n\nConsider speaking with a clinician soon if any of these happen:\n- The cough gets worse instead of slowly better, or you feel better and then get sicker again. The influenza summary notes that a new decline after several days of improvement can be a sign of a secondary bacterial pneumonia.\n- You have a high fever, or trouble breathing.\n- Your cold symptoms last ten days or more without improving, or you have severe symptoms with high fever and thick discharge. The stewardship guidance links these patterns to a possible bacterial sinus infection.\n- The cough keeps going well past three weeks, or it is worse at night. The asthma summary notes that night-time cough can be a sign of asthma, which needs a proper check to confirm.\n\nIf you have chest pain, coughing up blood, severe breathlessness, or confusion, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nThe sources I searched did not cover treatments for cough or what to do for your specific case, so please ask a pharmacist or clinician about symptom relief.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
