          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "Based on the antibiotic stewardship guidance, antibiotics generally would not help a cough that follows a cold. The guidance says that common colds and most sore throats and acute bronchitis are \"viral in the large majority of cases, and antibiotics do not shorten them\" (Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated). It also says antibiotics cause harm, including side effects, allergic reactions, and resistance. The guidance treats not prescribing them for these illnesses as the recommended approach.\n\nThe influenza summary says antibiotics have no effect on influenza. They are reserved for bacterial complications (Influenza). That is a separate illness, so it does not directly answer your question, but it fits the same pattern.\n\nThe guidance also says antibiotics may be considered if a bacterial complication develops. The search results did not describe which signs point to that, beyond the warning signs I listed earlier. Whether an antibiotic is right for you is a decision for a clinician who can examine you.\n\nThe search did not cover what treatments are used for cough or how to relieve it. A pharmacist or clinician can advise on that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
